-- 160 bits, FAILS (ok=0 fail=667). The 162 with the variable arm's skip
-- `list list u` (12 bits) replaced by a re-parse `list intL` (10 bits):
-- list intL = intL bit0 list1 = intL F list1, "the rest after the term at
-- list1". The recursion has no base case: intL F (1^k 0 ++ N) peels one 1 per
-- call and ends at intL F (0 ++ N) = N NEXT_F, where the terminating 0 is
-- read as a tag and the rigid N is destructured. Every index fails, not only
-- index 1. A base needs "0 ++ N is a complete term", which is the skip
-- function `list list j` itself, not the parser.
(\x. x x) (\x. (\intL. \cont. \list.
  list (\a. a) (\u. list (\q. cont) (list intL))
    (\bit1. intL (\exp. bit1
      (cont (\args \arg. exp (\zx \zy. zx arg (zy args))))
      (intL (\exp2. cont (\args. exp args (exp2 args)))))))
  (x x))
